# modules/cloud/aws/live/checks.rules - the §8.1 S3 read-only script-check
# registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md CLOUD-05).
# Owned by modules/cloud/aws/live/s3.sh; ids are all `CLOUD-S3-*`.
#
# THIS IS A SHARED, APPEND-ONLY REGISTRY FOR EVERY `aws/live/*.sh` SERVICE, and
# that is a decision rather than an accident.  rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path
# table reserves BOTH the `checks.rules` basename and the `checks-<name>.rules`
# spelling repository-wide, so a per-service `checks-s3.rules` here would be
# legal too - modules/dast/passive/ is split that way (docs/FOUNDATION.md
# tension 29).  It is NOT split here because tension 29's own argument is about
# CONFLICTS BETWEEN PARALLEL PEERS, and step 6's peers are dispatched as
# sequential bundles against one landed vertical slice rather than as a dozen
# simultaneous tickets: modules/dast/active/checks.rules and
# modules/dast/checks.rules are both under the identical append-only rule for
# the identical reason.  A CONFLICT IN THIS FILE IS RESOLVED BY KEEPING BOTH
# BLOCKS, never by choosing a side.  A later service whose peers really are
# in flight simultaneously may add its own `checks-<service>.rules` beside this
# one; both spellings load, because `checks_registry_load` (lib/checks.sh)
# globs every `*.rules` under `modules/cloud/` at any depth with no per-file
# allowlist and no registration step.
#
# `coverage-scope: account-region` is CLOUD's cell (§9.5.1), spelled
# `<account_id>/<region>` or `<account_id>/global`.  EVERY RECORD BELOW LANDS
# IN THE `<account_id>/global` CELL, and the reason is worth stating here
# rather than only in the script: `list-buckets` is a single account-wide call
# and `_CLOUD_SERVICES` marks `live/s3.sh` `global`, so the unit this run
# actually VISITED is the account's global S3 namespace - every bucket in the
# account, whatever region it sits in.  The bucket's own region is a fact about
# the RESOURCE and rides in the finding's `loc_region`, which is why a finding
# here cites a real region while its cell says `global`.  Writing the bucket's
# region into the cell instead would be the silent defect: coverage is recorded
# once per PASS (modules/cloud/aws/run.sh's `_cloud_record_coverage`), so a
# finding in a cell no pass ever covered can never be classified `fixed` and
# would sit at `unknown` forever - docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 12's whole point.
#
# SEVEN IDS FOR SIX BUCKET PROPERTIES, AND THE ONE SPLIT IS DELIBERATE.  The
# CLOUD location profile is (account_id, region, resource_key, sub_key) and
# carries no component naming the DEFECT, so two different problems on one
# bucket under one id would hash to one fingerprint and `findings_merge` would
# keep whichever sorted first - the identical argument
# modules/dast/passive/checks-cors.rules records for its own three ids.  The
# split that is NOT forced by the fingerprint is
# PUBLIC_ACL_READ vs PUBLIC_ACL_WRITE: it is two ids because SEVERITY VARIES
# WITH THE GRANTED PERMISSION, and `severity` is a per-record registry field
# (§9.5) that every suite asserts the script and the registry agree on - so a
# script that "weighted a finding higher" at runtime would put the two into
# disagreement.  Two ids also keeps the ordinary and the escalated case two
# findings rather than one whose meaning flips between runs, which is the same
# reasoning DAST-11's two `DAST-MARKUP-TABNABBING*` ids record.
#
# `cis:` IS AUTHORED HERE, ON THE RECORD, AND ONLY WHERE A REAL CIS v3.0.0
# CONTROL EXISTS.  data/cis-mappings is an id -> label table and never a source
# of ids (docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1).  CIS Amazon Web Services Foundations
# Benchmark v3.0.0 section 2.1 - the S3 section - contains exactly four
# controls (2.1.1 deny-HTTP bucket policy, 2.1.2 MFA Delete, 2.1.3 Macie
# discovery, 2.1.4 Block Public Access), so the four PUBLIC-EXPOSURE records
# below cite 2.1.4, the control whose enforcement each of them observes to be
# absent or defeated, and the three remaining records - default encryption,
# versioning, access logging - CITE NOTHING, because v3.0.0 has no S3 control
# for them.  (v1.4.0's 2.1.1 was "Ensure all S3 buckets employ encryption-at-
# rest"; v3.0.0 renumbered 2.1.1 to the deny-HTTP control after AWS made
# SSE-S3 a default, and citing the v1.4.0 number against a v3.0.0-declared
# table would misattribute the finding - §5 item 5 of docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md
# forbids exactly that.  3.4, "S3 bucket access logging is enabled on the
# CloudTrail S3 bucket", is about ONE named bucket and is not a general
# access-logging control, so CLOUD-S3-NO_LOGGING-01 does not cite it either.)
# An honest absence is the right outcome: `cis` is `optional, repeatable`
# (§9.5), an unmapped finding still carries its CWE and OWASP category, and
# inventing a control number to satisfy a "must cite CIS" habit is the
# overstated-coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 exists to forbid.  A future
# ticket that transcribes a control genuinely covering one of the three adds
# the row via docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §5 and the `cis:` line here in the same
# change.
#
# The type tag is `config-read` on every record: each check is one read-only
# AWS API call and mutates nothing, so lib/checks.sh's `--intensity` ceiling
# passes all of them at the default `passive` (its own type table names
# `config-read` in the `passive` row).  They also carry `quick`, which
# docs/DESIGN.md §5 defines as "passive + config-read, no active probes" -
# omitting it would leave `--profile-scan quick` selecting no cloud check at
# all.  `compliance` is carried by exactly the four records that carry a `cis`
# value, which is finding F3's TAG reading of that profile (lib/checks.sh).
# None is `intrusive`: there is no state to mutate, and `aws_ro` refuses a
# non-read operation with exit 3 before the CLI is even exec'd.

id: CLOUD-S3-PUBLIC_ACL_READ-01
title: S3 bucket ACL grants read access to a public grantee group
script: aws/live/s3.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 2.1.4
remediation: Remove the AllUsers ("everyone, including anonymous") or
  AuthenticatedUsers ("any AWS account holder anywhere, not just yours") grant
  from this bucket's ACL, then confirm nothing depended on it. Note that
  AuthenticatedUsers is not a narrowing - any person who can create a free AWS
  account is inside it. Prefer to disable ACLs entirely by setting the bucket's
  Object Ownership to BucketOwnerEnforced, which makes every ACL inert and
  leaves the bucket policy as the single access-control surface, and turn on
  all four Block Public Access settings at both the bucket and the account
  level so a future ACL cannot re-open it. If the objects really are meant to
  be world-readable, serve them through a CloudFront distribution with an
  Origin Access Control rather than by making the bucket itself public, so the
  bucket keeps a single, auditable reader.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/acl-overview.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-block-public-access.html

id: CLOUD-S3-PUBLIC_ACL_WRITE-01
title: S3 bucket ACL grants write or full control to a public grantee group
script: aws/live/s3.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 2.1.4
remediation: Remove this grant immediately. A WRITE, WRITE_ACP or FULL_CONTROL
  grant to AllUsers or AuthenticatedUsers lets an unauthenticated or
  arbitrary-AWS-account principal add, replace and delete objects in this
  bucket - and WRITE_ACP or FULL_CONTROL additionally lets them rewrite the
  ACL itself, so the exposure survives being partially undone. Set the
  bucket's Object Ownership to BucketOwnerEnforced to make every ACL inert,
  enable all four Block Public Access settings at the bucket and account
  level, and grant writes only to named principals through an IAM or bucket
  policy. Because a public writer may already have modified content, review
  the bucket's object versions and CloudTrail data-event history rather than
  assuming the grant was unused.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/acl-overview.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-block-public-access.html

id: CLOUD-S3-PUBLIC_POLICY-01
title: S3 bucket policy is evaluated as public by AWS
script: aws/live/s3.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 2.1.4
remediation: AWS's own policy evaluation reports this bucket's policy as
  public, which means at least one statement grants a bucket or object action
  to a principal that is not constrained to your account or organisation -
  typically Principal "*" with no Condition, or a Condition that does not
  actually narrow it (aws:Referer and aws:UserAgent are attacker-supplied and
  narrow nothing). Rewrite the statement to name the principals, accounts or
  aws:PrincipalOrgID that need the access, and enable the RestrictPublicBuckets
  and BlockPublicPolicy settings of Block Public Access so a future policy
  cannot re-open it. If the content is genuinely public, front it with a
  CloudFront distribution using an Origin Access Control instead of leaving the
  bucket itself reachable by any principal.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-block-public-access.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-policy-language-overview.html

id: CLOUD-S3-BLOCK_PUBLIC_ACCESS_OFF-01
title: S3 bucket Block Public Access is absent or partially disabled
script: aws/live/s3.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1188
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 2.1.4
fix-cli: aws s3api put-public-access-block --bucket %RESOURCE% --public-access-block-configuration BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true
remediation: Turn on all four Block Public Access settings for this bucket -
  BlockPublicAcls, IgnorePublicAcls, BlockPublicPolicy and
  RestrictPublicBuckets - and set the same four at the account level, which
  applies to every current and future bucket. The four are not
  interchangeable: the two Acls settings neutralise public ACLs, while
  BlockPublicPolicy rejects a new public bucket policy and
  RestrictPublicBuckets suppresses an existing one, so leaving any of them off
  leaves one route open. This finding is about the guardrail rather than about
  a bucket that is public today: with the guardrail off, one future ACL or
  policy edit is enough to expose the bucket, and nothing will refuse it.
references: CWE-1188
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-block-public-access.html

id: CLOUD-S3-NO_DEFAULT_ENCRYPTION-01
title: S3 bucket has no default server-side encryption configuration
script: aws/live/s3.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
fix-cli: aws s3api put-bucket-encryption --bucket %RESOURCE% --server-side-encryption-configuration '{"Rules":[{"ApplyServerSideEncryptionByDefault":{"SSEAlgorithm":"AES256"}}]}'
remediation: Set a default encryption configuration on the bucket so every
  object is encrypted at rest whether or not the uploading client asks for it.
  SSE-S3 (AES256) is the zero-cost baseline; SSE-KMS with a customer-managed
  key adds a separate, auditable key policy and CloudTrail key-usage events,
  which is what an estate needing separation of duties or a defined key
  rotation should use. Enable S3 Bucket Keys alongside SSE-KMS to keep the KMS
  request cost down. Note that default encryption applies to objects written
  after it is set, so also confirm whether existing objects need re-encrypting
  by copying them in place.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html

id: CLOUD-S3-NO_VERSIONING-01
title: S3 bucket versioning is not enabled
script: aws/live/s3.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
fix-cli: aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket %RESOURCE% --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled
remediation: Enable versioning on the bucket. Without it an overwrite or a
  delete is unrecoverable, which makes accidental deletion, a compromised
  credential and ransomware-style encryption of the bucket's contents all
  terminal rather than merely disruptive. With versioning on, add MFA Delete
  or an Object Lock retention policy if the data warrants it, and add a
  lifecycle rule expiring non-current versions so the storage cost stays
  bounded. A bucket whose versioning is Suspended is reported here as well as
  one that never had it: suspension stops new versions being created, so
  objects written from that point on are as unrecoverable as they were before
  versioning was ever enabled.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Versioning.html

id: CLOUD-S3-NO_LOGGING-01
title: S3 bucket server access logging is not enabled
script: aws/live/s3.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable server access logging (or CloudTrail S3 data events) for
  this bucket and send the records to a separate, restricted log bucket -
  never to the bucket being logged, which creates a feedback loop and lets
  anyone who can write to the source bucket also write its own audit trail.
  Without either, there is no record of who read or wrote which object, so a
  future investigation into a suspected exposure has nothing to work from and
  cannot even establish whether anything was accessed. Server access logs are
  best-effort and delivered on a delay; CloudTrail data events are the option
  to choose where the record must be reliable and queryable.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html

# docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md CLOUD-22 (`aws/live/apigw.sh`, docs/DESIGN.md §8.4).
# Two ids for one condition ("this method has no authorizer") for the same
# reason CLOUD-S3-PUBLIC_ACL_READ-01/WRITE-01 are two ids rather than one:
# `severity` is a per-record registry field every suite asserts the script and
# the registry agree on, and the two cases genuinely differ in severity - a
# route with NO barrier at all versus one gated by a shared, unauthenticated
# API key - so a script that "weighted a finding higher" at runtime would put
# the two into disagreement. CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 has no API
# Gateway section (its sections are IAM, S3, CloudTrail/Config/monitoring, KMS,
# RDS and VPC/networking only), so neither record carries a `cis:` value -
# docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1 forbids inventing a control id to satisfy a
# "must cite CIS" habit, and an honest absence is the correct outcome here.

id: CLOUD-APIGW-OPEN_AUTH_ROUTE-01
title: API Gateway method has no authorizer and no API key
script: aws/live/apigw.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-306
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Attach an authorizer to this method - AWS_IAM (SigV4-signed
  requests from an IAM principal), a Cognito user pool authorizer, or a
  Lambda (custom) authorizer - unless the route is genuinely meant to be
  public (a health check, a public content-delivery endpoint fronted by its
  own CDN-level controls). authorizationType NONE with apiKeyRequired false
  means API Gateway performs no check of any kind before invoking the
  integration: anyone who has this URL can call it. If some access control is
  wanted but a full authorizer is not yet available, apiKeyRequired alone is
  not a substitute - see CLOUD-APIGW-OPEN_AUTH_KEY_ONLY-01's own remediation
  for why.
references: CWE-306
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apigateway-control-access-to-api.html

id: CLOUD-APIGW-OPEN_AUTH_KEY_ONLY-01
title: API Gateway method has no authorizer and relies on an API key alone
script: aws/live/apigw.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-306
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: An API key is not an authorizer - API Gateway's apiKeyRequired
  setting only checks that SOME provisioned key was presented in the
  `x-api-key` header, never who the caller is, so every holder of any key
  provisioned anywhere in the account gets the identical access and a leaked
  or shared key cannot be traced to a caller. If callers need to be
  individually identified, revoked, or rate-limited per identity, attach a
  real authorizer (AWS_IAM, a Cognito user pool, or a Lambda authorizer) in
  addition to, or instead of, the key requirement. If this route is an
  intentional machine-to-machine integration where a shared key is an
  accepted risk, no change is needed beyond confirming the key is rotated and
  scoped to a usage plan.
references: CWE-306
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/api-gateway-api-usage-plans.html

# ===========================================================================
# CLOUD-07 (aws/live/kms.sh), CLOUD-08 (aws/live/secretsmanager.sh) and
# CLOUD-09 (aws/live/ssm.sh) - kms/secretsmanager/ssm, landed in one ticket
# and appended here together (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md).
#
# `cis:` FOLLOWS THE IDENTICAL DISCIPLINE THE S3 RECORDS ABOVE ESTABLISH: cited
# only where CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 names a real control, never
# invented to satisfy a "must cite CIS" habit (docs/DESIGN.md §15).  Of these
# six checks, exactly ONE has a real control: `CLOUD-KMS-ROTATION_DISABLED-01`
# cites 3.6 ("Ensure rotation for customer-created symmetric CMKs is
# enabled"), already present in data/cis-mappings.  The benchmark has no
# section for KMS key policies, Secrets Manager, or SSM Parameter Store at
# all - Security Hub's own public v3.0.0 mapping was checked and confirms
# none exists - so the other five carry no `cis:` line, an honest absence
# rather than a gap, the identical outcome the S3 encryption/versioning/
# logging records above already model.  A future benchmark revision that adds
# one of these sections gets its row added to data/cis-mappings and its
# `cis:` line added here, in the same change, per docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §5.
#
# All six are `config-read`/`quick`: each is one or more read-only AWS API
# calls with no active probe, exactly as the S3 records above.  Only
# `CLOUD-KMS-ROTATION_DISABLED-01` carries `compliance` (the tag `cis:`-bearing
# records use, per finding F3's TAG reading of `--profile-scan compliance`).

id: CLOUD-KMS-ROTATION_DISABLED-01
title: KMS customer-managed key does not have automatic rotation enabled
script: aws/live/kms.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-320
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 3.6
remediation: Enable automatic annual key rotation
  (`aws kms enable-key-rotation --key-id <id>`) for every customer-managed
  symmetric encryption key. AWS keeps every prior backing key version, so
  data encrypted before rotation still decrypts correctly and no application
  change is required. Rotation applies only to enabled, AWS_KMS-origin,
  SYMMETRIC_DEFAULT/ENCRYPT_DECRYPT keys - an asymmetric, HMAC, or
  imported-key-material key is out of scope for this control by design, not
  an oversight.
references: CWE-320
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/rotate-keys.html

id: CLOUD-KMS-PUBLIC_POLICY-01
title: KMS key policy grants to an unqualified wildcard principal
script: aws/live/kms.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the wildcard Principal (or Principal.AWS "*") from the
  key policy statement, or add a Condition that genuinely narrows it (an
  attacker-supplied context key such as aws:Referer narrows nothing). A KMS
  key policy is the ONLY access-control surface for the key - unlike an S3
  bucket, where IAM also gates access - so an unconditioned wildcard grant
  here means any AWS principal in any account that can name the key's ARN can
  request whatever this statement allows. Name the specific account(s),
  role(s), or aws:PrincipalOrgID that legitimately need access instead.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/key-policies.html

id: CLOUD-SECRETSMANAGER-NO_ROTATION-01
title: Secrets Manager secret does not have automatic rotation enabled
script: aws/live/secretsmanager.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-262
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Configure automatic rotation for the secret (a Lambda rotation
  function - AWS provides templates for RDS/DocumentDB/Redshift master
  credentials, and a custom function covers any other secret type) so a
  leaked or over-shared value has a bounded lifetime even when the leak
  itself is never detected. A secret owned by another AWS service (its
  OwningService field is set) or already scheduled for deletion is not
  evaluated by this check - its rotation lifecycle belongs to the owning
  service, not to this account's operator.
references: CWE-262
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_turn-on-for-other.html

id: CLOUD-SECRETSMANAGER-PUBLIC_POLICY-01
title: Secrets Manager resource policy grants to an unqualified wildcard principal
script: aws/live/secretsmanager.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the wildcard Principal from the secret's resource policy,
  or add a Condition that genuinely narrows it. A Secrets Manager resource
  policy controls who can call GetSecretValue directly, so an unconditioned
  wildcard grant means any AWS principal in any account that can name the
  secret's ARN can retrieve its value. Name the specific account(s) or
  role(s) that legitimately need access instead.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/auth-and-access_resource-based-policies.html

id: CLOUD-SSM-STRING_TYPE_SENSITIVE-01
title: SSM parameter name suggests sensitive content but is not a SecureString
script: aws/live/ssm.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-312
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Re-create the parameter with `--type SecureString`, which
  encrypts the value at rest with a KMS key and requires kms:Decrypt in
  addition to ssm:GetParameter to read it back. This is a NAME-based
  heuristic over the parameter's path (password/secret/token/credential/key),
  not a content scan: confirm the value is genuinely sensitive before acting,
  and note that a differently-named parameter holding a secret will not be
  caught by it.
references: CWE-312
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/parameter-store-securestring.html

id: CLOUD-SSM-PUBLIC_POLICY-01
title: SSM parameter resource policy grants to an unqualified wildcard principal
script: aws/live/ssm.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the wildcard Principal from the parameter's resource
  policy, or add a Condition that genuinely narrows it. An SSM resource
  policy controls cross-account access to the parameter directly, so an
  unconditioned wildcard grant means any AWS principal in any account that
  can name the parameter's ARN can call GetParameter against it. Name the
  specific account(s) or role(s) that legitimately need access instead.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/parameter-store-resource-policies.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUD-IAM-* - modules/cloud/aws/live/iam.sh (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md
# CLOUD-06).  Appended to this shared, append-only registry per the header
# note above; a merge conflict here is resolved by keeping BOTH blocks.
#
# EVERY RECORD BELOW LANDS IN THE `<account_id>/global` CELL, exactly as the
# CLOUD-S3-* records above do, and for an even simpler reason: IAM is
# genuinely global end to end, so `region: global` is not merely the cell -
# it is the literal `loc_region` this service's every finding carries
# (modules/cloud/aws/live/iam_engine.sh's own header explains why that
# differs from S3, whose bucket-region resolution keeps the two apart).
#
# `cis:` IS AUTHORED HERE, ON THE RECORD, AND ONLY WHERE data/cis-mappings
# ALREADY CARRIES THE CONTROL (docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1: it is a label table,
# never a source of ids).  Eight of these twelve checks cite a seeded CIS
# v3.0.0 control from IAM section 1: 1.4 (root access key),
# 1.5 (root MFA), 1.8 and 1.9 (the password-policy minimum length and reuse
# prevention - both cited on the one WEAK_PASSWORD_POLICY record, since an
# absent policy fails both simultaneously and the check has no way to tell
# them apart from a policy that fails only one), 1.12 (credentials unused 45+
# days), 1.14 (access keys rotated every 90 days or less), and 1.20 (IAM
# Access Analyzer enabled). The remaining four - full-admin policies, a
# wildcard/cross-account trust relationship, an unused role, and inline/
# managed policy sprawl - cite NOTHING: CIS v3.0.0 control 1.16 (full-admin
# policies) is real but data/cis-mappings has no row for it yet (a stated
# gap, docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §4 - a future ticket transcribes it, per that
# document's §5, rather than this one guessing at a number with no PDF in
# hand), and the benchmark has no numbered control at all for a wildcard
# trust principal, a missing sts:ExternalId, an unused role, or mixed
# inline/managed policies. An honest absence beats an invented control id;
# every one of these four still carries its own CWE and OWASP category.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `config-read` ON EVERY RECORD, exactly as CLOUD-S3-*'s own
# note explains: each check is one read-only AWS API call (or a small,
# bounded sequence of them) and mutates nothing, so lib/checks.sh's
# `--intensity` ceiling passes all of them at the default `passive`. They
# also carry `quick`, and `compliance` on the eight that carry a `cis` value -
# finding F3's TAG reading of that profile. None is `intrusive`.

id: CLOUD-IAM-ROOT_MFA_OFF-01
title: The AWS account root user has no MFA device registered
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-308
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 1.5
remediation: Register a hardware or virtual MFA device for the root user
  under IAM > My Security Credentials, then use root only for the handful of
  tasks that genuinely require it - creating the account's first IAM
  administrator, closing the account, and the small set of actions AWS
  documents as root-only. The root user cannot be restricted by any IAM
  policy, so a compromised root credential with no second factor is an
  immediate, unrestrictable account takeover.
references: CWE-308
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_root-user.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-ROOT_ACCESS_KEY-01
title: The AWS account root user has an active access key
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 1.4
remediation: Delete every root access key under IAM > My Security
  Credentials. The root user should never be used for programmatic access;
  any automation that currently depends on a root key needs a named IAM role
  or user with least-privilege permissions instead. Rotate any credential the
  key was used to protect once the key is deleted, since its prior use cannot
  be retroactively distinguished from misuse.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_root-user.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-WEAK_PASSWORD_POLICY-01
title: The account's IAM password policy is absent or below the CIS minimums
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-521
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 1.8
cis: 1.9
remediation: Set an account password policy under IAM > Account settings >
  Password policy requiring a minimum length of at least 14 characters and
  remembering at least the last 24 passwords, matching CIS v3.0.0 controls
  1.8 and 1.9. An account with no password policy at all fails both at once.
references: CWE-521
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_passwords_account-policy.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-ACCESS_ANALYZER_DISABLED-01
title: No active IAM Access Analyzer was found for this account
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 1.20
remediation: Enable IAM Access Analyzer under IAM > Access Analyzer, in every
  region this account uses (CIS v3.0.0 control 1.20 asks for all of them,
  though this check itself examines only the region the run's own AWS CLI
  configuration resolves - see modules/cloud/aws/live/iam.sh's own header).
  Access Analyzer flags resources whose own resource policy makes them
  reachable from outside the account or organization, a cross-resource class
  of exposure no single-resource read-only check in this catalog can detect
  on its own.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/what-is-access-analyzer.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-POLICY_FULL_ADMIN-01
title: An IAM identity carries a policy granting full administrative privileges
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Replace the statement granting Effect Allow, Action "*",
  Resource "*" with one scoped to the specific actions and resources this
  identity actually needs. This check matches CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark
  v3.0.0 control 1.16's own published audit shape for a policy document, not
  a live policy simulation - an equivalent permission set assembled from
  several narrower-looking statements will not be caught by this pattern, so
  a clean result here is not a complete privilege review on its own.
references: CWE-269
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-TRUST_WILDCARD_PRINCIPAL-01
title: An IAM role's trust policy admits any AWS principal
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Replace the wildcard Principal with the specific account ids,
  role or user ARNs, or AWS service principals that legitimately need to
  assume this role. A trust policy is the ONLY control standing between "any
  AWS principal in existence" and everything this role's own permissions
  reach.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_terms-and-concepts.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-TRUST_NO_EXTERNAL_ID-01
title: An IAM role's trust policy allows a cross-account principal with no ExternalId condition
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-441
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Add a Condition requiring a unique, secret sts:ExternalId to
  every statement that trusts an AWS account other than this one, per AWS's
  own third-party cross-account access guidance. Without one, a trust
  relationship reused across more than one external customer of the trusting
  account is the confused-deputy pattern that guidance exists to prevent -
  one customer's request assuming a role meant for another's resources.
references: CWE-441
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_create_for-user_externalid.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-UNUSED_CREDENTIAL-01
title: An IAM credential has not been used in 45 days or more
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-613
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 1.12
remediation: Disable or delete the console password or access key named in
  this finding's evidence. A credential nobody is using is a credential
  nobody will notice if it is stolen, and it contributes nothing that
  removing it would take away.
references: CWE-613
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_getting-report.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-ACCESS_KEY_ROTATION-01
title: An IAM access key is 90 days old or older and has not been rotated
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-262
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 1.14
remediation: Create a replacement access key, update whatever uses the old
  one to the new key, confirm the new key works, then delete the old one. A
  long-lived static credential has a longer window in which a leak goes
  undetected, and rotating on a schedule bounds that window regardless of
  whether a leak is ever suspected.
references: CWE-262
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_access-keys.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-UNUSED_ROLE-01
title: An IAM role has not been assumed in 90 days or more
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Confirm nothing still depends on the role named in this
  finding's evidence and remove it if not. An unused role is standing access
  with no active owner relying on it, and every additional role is one more
  trust policy and permission set that has to be reviewed on every future
  audit whether or not anything still uses it.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_manage.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-NO_PERMISSION_BOUNDARY-01
title: A full-admin IAM identity has no permission boundary attached
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Attach a permission boundary to the identity named in this
  finding's evidence, scoped to the maximum permissions it should ever be
  able to reach. A boundary caps the damage of both a compromised credential
  and a future policy change that widens the identity's own attached policy
  without anyone revisiting whether it still should be full-admin.
references: CWE-269
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_boundaries.html

id: CLOUD-IAM-POLICY_SPRAWL-01
title: An IAM identity mixes inline and attached managed policies
script: aws/live/iam.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-710
owasp: A04:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Consolidate the identity named in this finding's evidence onto
  managed (ideally customer-managed) policies, and remove its inline policies
  once their grants are represented there. An identity whose permissions are
  split between an inline policy and one or more attached managed policies
  has no single place a reviewer can read to see its whole effective
  permission set.
references: CWE-710
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_managed-vs-inline.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EC2/VPC (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md CLOUD-13) - owned by
# modules/cloud/aws/live/ec2.sh, ids all `CLOUD-EC2-*`.  Appended to this
# shared, append-only registry per this file's own header note - a per-service
# `checks-ec2.rules` would also be legal (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path
# table admits both spellings), but step 6's tickets are sequential bundles
# against one landed vertical slice rather than parallel peers, so the
# tension-29 split modules/dast/passive/ needed does not apply here.
#
# EIGHT IDS FOR SEVEN PROPERTIES.  The admin-port and database-port cases are
# two ids, `SG_OPEN_ADMIN_PORT` and `SG_OPEN_DB_PORT`, because CIS AWS
# Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 control 5.2 covers "remote server
# administration ports" (22, 3389) specifically and has no database-port
# equivalent - citing 5.2 against a MySQL/Redis/... exposure would
# misattribute a control that does not cover it (docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1),
# and the CLOUD location profile carries no component naming which port
# family fired, so one id would also collide the two onto one fingerprint.
#
# `cis:` IS AUTHORED HERE, ON THE RECORD, AND ONLY WHERE A REAL CIS v3.0.0
# CONTROL EXISTS (data/cis-mappings is an id -> label table, never a source of
# ids - docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1).  Four of these eight cite a real control
# (5.2, 5.4, 5.6, 3.7, all already seeded in data/cis-mappings for exactly
# this v1 CIS-core service scope); the other four - the database-port case,
# public AMIs, public EBS snapshots, and unencrypted volumes - cite nothing,
# because CIS v3.0.0 has no control for any of them.  An honest absence is the
# right outcome; inventing a control number to satisfy a "must cite CIS"
# habit is the overstated-coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 forbids.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `config-read` ON EVERY RECORD: each check is one or more
# read-only `describe-*`/`get-*` AWS API calls and mutates nothing, so
# lib/checks.sh's `--intensity` ceiling passes all of them at the default
# `passive`. They also carry `quick` (docs/DESIGN.md §5's "passive +
# config-read, no active probes"); `compliance` is carried by exactly the
# four records that carry a `cis` value (finding F3's TAG reading of that
# profile). None is `intrusive`: there is no state to mutate, and `aws_ro`
# refuses a non-read operation with exit 3 before the CLI is even exec'd.

id: CLOUD-EC2-SG_OPEN_ADMIN_PORT-01
title: Security group allows ingress from 0.0.0.0/0 to a remote administration port
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 5.2
remediation: Remove the 0.0.0.0/0 ingress rule for port 22 (SSH) or 3389
  (RDP) from this security group. A rule this broad lets any host on the
  internet attempt to authenticate against the port, which is one of the
  most commonly scanned and brute-forced surfaces on AWS. Restrict ingress to
  a known jump host or bastion's own security group, a VPN or Direct Connect
  CIDR, or replace the direct exposure entirely with AWS Systems Manager
  Session Manager, which needs no inbound rule at all.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html

id: CLOUD-EC2-SG_OPEN_DB_PORT-01
title: Security group allows ingress from 0.0.0.0/0 to a common database port
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the 0.0.0.0/0 ingress rule for this database port
  (MySQL/MariaDB 3306, PostgreSQL 5432, MSSQL 1433/1434, Oracle 1521, MongoDB
  27017, Redis 6379, CouchDB 5984, Elasticsearch 9200, Memcached 11211, or
  Redshift 5439). A database reachable from the whole internet is exposed to
  credential-stuffing and unpatched-CVE scanning from the moment it is
  provisioned, regardless of how strong its own authentication is. Restrict
  ingress to the application tier's own security group and place the database
  in a private subnet with no route to the internet.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html

id: CLOUD-EC2-DEFAULT_SG_IN_USE-01
title: The VPC's default security group is attached to a resource
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1188
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 5.4
remediation: Move every resource attached to the VPC's default security
  group onto a purpose-specific security group whose rules are reviewed
  deliberately, then remove all of the default security group's own ingress
  and egress rules so it restricts all traffic, per CIS AWS Foundations
  Benchmark control 5.4. A resource left in the default group inherits
  whatever rule set it carries and is the group most likely to be overlooked
  in a review that only inspects custom security groups by name.
references: CWE-1188
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html#default-security-group

id: CLOUD-EC2-PUBLIC_AMI-01
title: Owned AMI grants public launch permission
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the AMI's public launch permission (the AllUsers/"all"
  group in describe-image-attribute's launchPermission) immediately. Any AWS
  account in the partition can launch an instance from a publicly-shared AMI
  and inspect its full disk contents, which routinely includes application
  code, configuration files, and any credential baked into the image at build
  time. Share the AMI only with named account ids, or with an AWS
  Organization/Resource Access Manager share where the recipient set needs to
  be broad but still bounded.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/sharingamis-explicit.html

id: CLOUD-EC2-PUBLIC_EBS_SNAPSHOT-01
title: Owned EBS snapshot grants public create-volume permission
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the snapshot's public create-volume permission (the
  AllUsers/"all" group in describe-snapshot-attribute's
  createVolumePermission) immediately. Any AWS account in the partition can
  create a volume from a publicly-shared snapshot, attach it to their own
  instance, and read every byte it contains - a direct data-exposure path
  that bypasses every access control the original, running instance ever
  had. Share the snapshot only with named account ids, and consider whether
  any data it contains needs to be treated as already exposed.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ebs-modifying-snapshot-permissions.html

id: CLOUD-EC2-UNENCRYPTED_VOLUME-01
title: EBS volume is not encrypted at rest
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable "EBS encryption by default" for the region so every
  volume created from now on is encrypted automatically, then replace this
  volume with an encrypted copy - create a snapshot, copy it with
  encryption enabled (or copy an unencrypted snapshot into an encrypted one
  directly), and create a new volume from the encrypted copy, since an
  existing volume cannot be encrypted in place. Without encryption at rest, a
  compromised storage-layer credential or a mis-shared snapshot exposes the
  volume's contents in plaintext.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/EBSEncryption.html

id: CLOUD-EC2-IMDSV2_NOT_ENFORCED-01
title: EC2 instance metadata service does not enforce IMDSv2
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-918
owasp: A10:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 5.6
remediation: Set the instance's metadata options to HttpTokens required
  (IMDSv2 only), per CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark control 5.6. The original
  metadata service (IMDSv1) answers a plain, unauthenticated HTTP GET from
  inside the instance, so any request the instance can be tricked into
  making on its own behalf - the textbook Server-Side Request Forgery shape -
  can retrieve the instance's own IAM role credentials from
  169.254.169.254. IMDSv2 requires a session token obtained via a PUT
  request first, which most SSRF techniques cannot forge.
references: CWE-918
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/configuring-instance-metadata-service.html

id: CLOUD-EC2-FLOW_LOGS_OFF-01
title: VPC has no active flow log
script: aws/live/ec2.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 3.7
remediation: Enable VPC flow logs, per CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark control
  3.7, delivering them to CloudWatch Logs or S3 with a retention period
  matched to the estate's incident-response window. Without a flow log there
  is no record of which network traffic was accepted or rejected in or out
  of the VPC, so an investigation into a suspected compromise or a
  data-exfiltration path has no network-layer evidence to work from -
  identical in kind to the S3 access-logging gap this project's own
  CLOUD-S3-NO_LOGGING-01 check reports one layer up the stack.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/flow-logs.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The §8.1 governance & detection bundle (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md
# CLOUD-30..34), appended to this shared, append-only registry per this
# file's own header rule.  Five services, seven checks:
# `modules/cloud/aws/live/cloudtrail.sh` (three - not enabled, not
# multi-region, log file validation off), `config.sh`, `guardduty.sh`,
# `inspector.sh` and `macie.sh` (one each).
#
# `cis:` IS AUTHORED ONLY WHERE A REAL CIS v3.0.0 CONTROL EXISTS, per this
# file's own rule above.  CloudTrail cites 3.1 (twice, for its two distinct
# failure modes - "no trail" and "no multi-region trail" are both facts
# about the same control) and 3.2; AWS Config cites 3.3; all three already
# have rows in data/cis-mappings, so no new mapping entry was needed.
# GuardDuty, Inspector2 and Macie2 have NO CIS Amazon Web Services
# Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 control anywhere in that benchmark's sections
# 1-5 (confirmed against data/cis-mappings' own scope, and its header's own
# statement that section 4 - the closest thing to a detection-service
# section - is entirely "Not supported - manual check" for v3.0.0 and
# carries no control ids this table could cite). Their three records
# therefore carry no `cis:` line and no `compliance` tag - an honest
# absence, not a gap to be quietly filled with an invented number
# (docs/DESIGN.md §15).
#
# Every record is `config-read`/`quick`, the identical reasoning the S3
# records above give: each check is one or two read-only AWS API calls and
# mutates nothing.

id: CLOUD-CLOUDTRAIL-NOT_ENABLED-01
title: CloudTrail is not logging in this account or region
script: aws/live/cloudtrail.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 3.1
remediation: Create a CloudTrail trail (or restart logging on an existing
  one with StartLogging) so API activity in this account is recorded. A
  single, enabled, multi-region trail with log file validation on is the
  minimum an account needs before any of the rest of this bundle's checks -
  or an incident investigation - has anything to work from. Without it,
  there is no audit record of who did what in this account at all.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail.html

id: CLOUD-CLOUDTRAIL-NOT_MULTI_REGION-01
title: CloudTrail trail is not multi-region
script: aws/live/cloudtrail.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 3.1
remediation: Convert this trail to multi-region (UpdateTrail
  --is-multi-region-trail), or create a separate multi-region trail
  alongside it. A single-region trail records activity only in its own home
  region, so an action taken through any other region's endpoint - which
  includes almost every global-service API call and any region an attacker
  chooses specifically because it is not being watched - leaves no trace at
  all. CIS 3.1 requires at least one enabled, logging, multi-region trail.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail.html

id: CLOUD-CLOUDTRAIL-LOG_FILE_VALIDATION_OFF-01
title: CloudTrail log file validation is not enabled
script: aws/live/cloudtrail.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-345
owasp: A08:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 3.2
remediation: Enable log file validation on the trail
  (UpdateTrail --enable-log-file-validation). Each delivered log file is
  then paired with a signed digest file, so a log file modified or deleted
  after delivery - by an attacker covering their tracks, or by a
  misconfigured retention policy - can be detected rather than trusted
  silently. Without it, a CloudTrail log is evidence only until someone with
  write access to the log bucket decides it should not be.
references: CWE-345
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-log-file-validation-intro.html

id: CLOUD-CONFIG-RECORDER_OFF-01
title: AWS Config configuration recorder is absent or not recording
script: aws/live/config.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 3.3
remediation: Create a configuration recorder covering all resource types
  (PutConfigurationRecorder with recordingGroup.allSupported true) and start
  it (StartConfigurationRecorder) in this region. Without an active
  recorder, AWS Config is not tracking how resource configuration in this
  region changes over time, which removes both the change-history an
  incident investigation needs and the input every AWS Config rule in this
  region depends on to evaluate anything at all.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/config/latest/developerguide/gs-console.html

id: CLOUD-GUARDDUTY-DISABLED-01
title: GuardDuty is not enabled in this region
script: aws/live/guardduty.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable GuardDuty in this region (CreateDetector, or
  UpdateDetector to set an existing detector's Enable to true). GuardDuty is
  this account's automated analysis of CloudTrail management events, VPC
  Flow Logs and DNS query logs for known malicious and anomalous activity
  patterns; with it off, that analysis simply does not happen, and a
  compromised credential or instance in this region will not generate a
  finding here however long it is active.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/what-is-guardduty.html

id: CLOUD-INSPECTOR-DISABLED-01
title: Inspector2 automated vulnerability scanning is not fully enabled
script: aws/live/inspector.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A06:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable Inspector2 scanning for every resource type this
  account actually uses in this region (Enable, naming the affected
  resourceTypes - EC2, ECR, LAMBDA). Without it, a newly-disclosed CVE
  against an in-use EC2 AMI's installed packages, an ECR container image's
  layers, or a Lambda function's dependencies is not detected by anything
  this account runs on its own; it is found only when something external
  does - an attacker, a customer, or a researcher's disclosure.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/enabling.html

id: CLOUD-MACIE-DISABLED-01
title: Macie sensitive-data discovery is not enabled in this region
script: aws/live/macie.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable Macie in this region (EnableMacie), then configure a
  discovery job against the S3 buckets this account uses. Without it, no
  automated scan is looking for PII, credentials or financial data sitting
  in an S3 bucket that should not hold it, so that kind of exposure is found
  only by accident or after it has already been used against this account.
  Note this check's confidence is medium rather than high: Macie reports
  "not enabled" as the same AccessDeniedException code a genuine permission
  gap on the scanning role's own IAM policy would produce (see
  modules/cloud/aws/live/macie.sh's own header), so an absent
  macie2:GetMacieSession grant on an otherwise-enabled account can read the
  same way as Macie genuinely being off.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/macie-setting-up.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owned by modules/cloud/aws/live/lambda.sh (docs/DESIGN.md §8.6;
# docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md CLOUD-21). Ids are all `CLOUD-LAMBDA-*`, appended
# to this SHARED, APPEND-ONLY registry rather than a per-service
# `checks-lambda.rules` - this file's own header above already states why:
# step 6's peers are dispatched as sequential bundles against one landed
# vertical slice, not as parallel tickets fighting over one file, which is
# the CONFLICT tension 29's per-owner split exists to prevent and which does
# not apply here.
#
# `coverage-scope: account-region` is CLOUD's cell, exactly as the S3 records
# above declare - but UNLIKE `s3`, whose `list-buckets` call is account-wide
# (a `global` row in `_CLOUD_SERVICES`), `lambda` is a REGIONAL row: `lambda
# list-functions` answers only for the region it is addressed to, so every
# finding below lands in the `<account_id>/<region>` cell the pass actually
# ran in, and that region IS the function's own region - there is no
# S3-shaped split between "the cell the pass covered" and "the resource's own
# region" here, because for a regional service they are the same fact.
#
# SIX IDS FOR THREE PROPERTIES, splitting each of docs/DESIGN.md §8.6's three
# bullets in two for the identical reason S3's own PUBLIC_ACL_READ/WRITE
# split states: the CLOUD location profile carries no component naming the
# DEFECT, so two different problems on one function under one id would
# collide onto one fingerprint and `findings_merge` would keep whichever
# sorted first, and each pair's two halves genuinely differ in SEVERITY - a
# per-record registry field a script cannot "weigh" at runtime without
# putting itself in disagreement with the record.
#   - ROLE_WILDCARD (critical) vs ROLE_SENSITIVE_SERVICE (high): an
#     account-wide-admin-equivalent grant (`Action:"*"`, `Resource:"*"`) is a
#     strictly worse root cause than a wildcard confined to one sensitive
#     service, and reporting a role that happens to trip BOTH readings under
#     one id would understate the narrower, commoner case's own severity.
#   - PUBLIC_FUNCTION_URL (critical) vs PUBLIC_POLICY (critical/pattern): two
#     independent SINKS for the same "unauthenticated invocation" root cause
#     - a function URL and a resource-based policy are two different API
#       surfaces a function can be reached through, and either can exist
#       without the other - the same "one root cause, two sinks, two ids"
#       shape modules/dast/active/hosthdr.sh's own two check ids record.
#   - ENV_SECRET (high, confidence medium - a NAME/VALUE-shape heuristic, not
#     a proof) vs ENV_NOT_ENCRYPTED (low): a credential sitting in an env var
#     is a live exposure the moment anyone can call
#     lambda:GetFunctionConfiguration; the absence of a customer-managed KMS
#     key protecting the whole block is a weaker, defence-in-depth gap that
#     exists whether or not any single value in it happens to be a secret.
#
# `cis:` IS AUTHORED HERE, ON THE RECORD, exactly as the S3 header above
# states - and NONE of the six records below carries one. CIS AWS Foundations
# Benchmark v3.0.0 has no dedicated Lambda section at all (its five sections
# are IAM, Storage, Logging, Monitoring, Networking - data/cis-mappings'
# own 34-row seed, cross-referenced against AWS Security Hub's public v3.0.0
# control-id mapping, names none for Lambda), so there is no control an
# honest transcription could cite. This is the identical "an honest absence
# is the right outcome" call the S3 header above makes for its own three
# unmapped checks (default encryption, versioning, logging) - inventing a
# control number to satisfy a "must cite CIS" habit is the overstated-
# coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 forbids. A future CIS version that adds
# a Lambda section, or a benchmark cross-reference that surfaces an existing
# IAM control genuinely covering one of these six, adds the row via
# docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §5 and the `cis:` line here in the same change - none
# is `compliance`-tagged for the identical reason none carries a `cis` value
# (S3's header states the TAG reading of the `compliance` profile: only a
# check with a real `cis` value earns the tag).
#
# The type tag is `config-read` on every record, exactly as the S3 records
# above: each check is one or more read-only AWS API calls (list-functions,
# list-function-url-configs, get-policy, and - for the two role checks -
# list-role-policies/get-role-policy/list-attached-role-policies/get-policy/
# get-policy-version) and mutates nothing, so `--intensity`'s ceiling passes
# all six at the default `passive`. They also carry `quick`
# (docs/DESIGN.md §5's "passive + config-read, no active probes").

id: CLOUD-LAMBDA-ROLE_WILDCARD-01
title: Lambda execution role grants unrestricted Action "*" on Resource "*"
script: aws/live/lambda.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-250
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Replace the offending policy statement with the specific actions
  and resource ARNs this function's code actually calls - AWS IAM Access
  Analyzer's policy generation, run against the role's own recent CloudTrail
  activity, is the fastest path to a scoped replacement. An `Action: "*"` on
  `Resource: "*"` grant gives this function's code, any dependency it loads,
  and anything that can influence its input the same reach as an account
  administrator; there is no narrower control left to fail once this one has.
references: CWE-250
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-permissions.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access-analyzer-policy-generation.html

id: CLOUD-LAMBDA-ROLE_SENSITIVE_SERVICE-01
title: Lambda execution role grants a wildcard action on a sensitive AWS service
script: aws/live/lambda.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-250
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Narrow the flagged `<service>:*` action to the specific calls
  this function actually makes, and confine the Resource element to the
  specific ARNs it needs rather than leaving it unrestricted. Which services
  count as sensitive enough to flag on a bare wildcard alone - identity
  (iam), key management (kms), secrets (secretsmanager), cross-identity
  assumption (sts), the organization itself (organizations), and networking
  primitives (ec2) - is a configurable, operator-editable list in
  modules/cloud/aws/live/lambda-sensitive-services.txt; a function that
  genuinely needs broad access to one of these should still narrow the
  Resource element even where the Action list cannot shrink further.
references: CWE-250
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-permissions.html

id: CLOUD-LAMBDA-PUBLIC_FUNCTION_URL-01
title: Lambda function URL allows unauthenticated invocation (AuthType NONE)
script: aws/live/lambda.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set the function URL's AuthType to AWS_IAM and grant
  lambda:InvokeFunctionUrl only to the specific principals that need to call
  it; a NONE AuthType means any request that reaches the URL over the
  internet invokes the function with no AWS authentication at all. If
  anonymous access is genuinely required for a subset of callers, front the
  URL with an authenticating layer (API Gateway with its own authorizer, or
  CloudFront with signed requests/URLs) rather than leaving the raw function
  URL open.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/urls-auth.html

id: CLOUD-LAMBDA-PUBLIC_POLICY-01
title: Lambda resource-based policy allows a wildcard or unrestricted principal
script: aws/live/lambda.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Replace the "*" (or {"AWS":"*"}) principal with the specific
  account, role, or AWS-service principal that genuinely needs to invoke this
  function, or add a Condition (aws:SourceArn / aws:SourceAccount) that
  narrows the grant to a specific caller if one is not already present.
  Confidence is medium rather than high because Lambda has no
  server-evaluated "is this policy public" API the way S3's
  get-bucket-policy-status gives S3 - this is a pattern match over the
  policy's Principal element, and a real Condition on the flagged statement
  may already narrow it; review the statement before assuming the grant is
  unconditionally public.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/access-control-resource-based.html

id: CLOUD-LAMBDA-ENV_SECRET-01
title: Lambda environment variable looks like a plaintext credential
script: aws/live/lambda.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Move this value out of the function's environment variables and
  into AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString), reading it
  at invocation time with the minimum IAM grant needed to fetch that one
  secret - an environment variable is visible in plaintext, via
  lambda:GetFunctionConfiguration, to every principal permitted to read the
  function's configuration, which is routinely a broader set than the ones
  the credential is meant to be scoped to. Confidence is medium because this
  is a name- or shape-based heuristic (modules/cloud/aws/live/lambda-secret-env-keywords.txt),
  not a proof that the value is genuinely live; the finding's evidence never
  includes the value itself.
references: CWE-798
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-envvars.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/retrieving-secrets_lambda.html

id: CLOUD-LAMBDA-ENV_NOT_ENCRYPTED-01
title: Lambda environment variables are not encrypted with a customer-managed key
script: aws/live/lambda.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Configure a customer-managed KMS key for this function's
  environment-variable encryption. Lambda always encrypts environment
  variables at rest, so this finding is not "unencrypted" in the literal
  sense - it is that the AWS-owned default key protecting them cannot be
  rotated on your own schedule, restricted with its own key policy, or have
  its usage audited separately from every other function relying on that
  same shared default key. This finding is only raised for a function that
  has at least one environment variable configured.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-envvars.html#configuration-envvars-encryption

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUD-14 (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md) - modules/cloud/aws/live/elb.sh.
# `elb` (Classic Load Balancer) and `elbv2` (ALB/NLB) are one AWS product,
# "Elastic Load Balancing", across two API generations
# (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md's own counting note) - one script, one set of
# check ids, evaluated against both namespaces.  `elb.sh` is a `regional` row
# in `_CLOUD_SERVICES`, unlike S3's `global` one, so `loc_region` here is
# simply the region the pass ran in - see elb_engine.sh's own header for why
# that needs no second resolution the way an S3 bucket's does.
#
# NO `cis:` ON ANY OF THESE THREE.  CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 has
# no Elastic Load Balancing section at all - its scope is IAM (section 1),
# storage encryption/access (2), logging (3) and networking (5); confirmed
# against data/cis-mappings, which transcribes the full v1(CIS-core) scope
# and carries no `CLOUD-ELB-*`-shaped row.  Per docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1,
# `data/cis-mappings` is an id -> label table and never a source of ids: an
# absent `cis:` line here is the honest reading, exactly as it is for S3's own
# NO_DEFAULT_ENCRYPTION/NO_VERSIONING/NO_LOGGING records above, and inventing
# a control number to satisfy a "must cite CIS" habit is the
# overstated-coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 forbids.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

id: CLOUD-ELB-HTTP_NO_REDIRECT-01
title: Load balancer listener accepts plain HTTP with no redirect to HTTPS
script: aws/live/elb.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: A Classic Load Balancer has no redirect capability at all - it
  is a pass-through TCP/HTTP proxy - so remove the plain-HTTP listener
  entirely, or migrate to an Application Load Balancer where a redirect
  action can be configured. On an ALB, add or fix the HTTP listener's default
  action so it is a `redirect` whose `Protocol` is exactly `HTTPS` (not the
  `#{protocol}` placeholder, which preserves the original scheme and performs
  no upgrade at all) rather than forwarding the request in the clear.
references: CWE-319
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/create-https-listener.html

id: CLOUD-ELB-WEAK_TLS_POLICY-01
title: Load balancer HTTPS/TLS listener uses a TLS policy that does not prove a TLS 1.2 floor
script: aws/live/elb.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-326
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Attach a security policy that names a TLS 1.2 (or later) floor -
  on ALB/NLB, one of the `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS-1-2-*` or
  `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-*` predefined policies (Forward-Secrecy variants
  where the client population supports them); on a Classic Load Balancer,
  replace the listener's policy with `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS-1-2-2017-01` or
  newer, or a custom policy that explicitly disables SSLv3, TLSv1 and
  TLSv1.1. On ALB/NLB the check reads the listener's own `SslPolicy` field
  directly, so a CUSTOM policy name that does not itself declare a TLS 1.2+
  floor is reported even when the underlying cipher/protocol list happens to
  be fine - see elb_engine.sh's `elb_policy_is_weak`, which proves strong
  rather than matches a known-weak name. On a Classic Load Balancer the check
  instead resolves the attached policy's own attributes
  (`Reference-Security-Policy`, or the `Protocol-SSLv3`/`Protocol-TLSv1`/
  `Protocol-TLSv1.1` flags on a fully custom policy), because a Classic ELB
  listener's own `PolicyNames` entry is an operator-chosen label that
  routinely carries no protocol information at all, unlike ALB/NLB's field.
references: CWE-326
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/describe-ssl-policies.html

id: CLOUD-ELB-NO_ACCESS_LOGS-01
title: Load balancer access logging is not enabled
script: aws/live/elb.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable access logging for this load balancer (the
  `access_logs.s3.enabled` attribute on ALB/NLB, or the `AccessLog.Enabled`
  attribute on a Classic Load Balancer) and deliver the logs to a separate,
  restricted S3 bucket. Without it there is no per-request record of who was
  served by this load balancer, so a later investigation into a suspected
  abuse or outage has nothing to work from.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/load-balancer-access-logs.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUD-24 (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md) - modules/cloud/aws/live/cloudfront.sh.
# `cloudfront` is a `global` row in `_CLOUD_SERVICES`, and every finding here
# cites `region: global` - a CloudFront distribution has no per-resource AWS
# region at all (cloudfront_engine.sh's own header). No `cis:` on any of
# these five, for the identical reason the CLOUD-ELB-* records above give:
# CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 has no CloudFront section, and
# data/cis-mappings carries no row for one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

id: CLOUD-CLOUDFRONT-VIEWER_HTTP_ALLOWED-01
title: CloudFront distribution permits plain HTTP between viewer and edge
script: aws/live/cloudfront.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set the distribution's (default and any per-path) cache
  behavior ViewerProtocolPolicy to `redirect-to-https` or `https-only`
  instead of `allow-all`. With `allow-all`, a viewer that happens to request
  the plain-HTTP URL is served over an unencrypted connection between itself
  and the CloudFront edge, and nothing about the distribution's own
  configuration prevents that request from ever being made.
references: CWE-319
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/using-https-viewers-to-cloudfront.html

id: CLOUD-CLOUDFRONT-WEAK_MIN_TLS-01
title: CloudFront distribution's minimum viewer TLS protocol version is weak
script: aws/live/cloudfront.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-326
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Attach a certificate through AWS Certificate Manager (a
  distribution using the shared CloudFront default certificate cannot set a
  custom minimum protocol version at all, and its fixed minimum is weak) and
  set the distribution's SecurityPolicy/MinimumProtocolVersion to
  `TLSv1.2_2021` or later, so a client attempting a connection below TLS 1.2
  is refused rather than served.
references: CWE-326
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/secure-connections-supported-viewer-protocols-ciphers.html

id: CLOUD-CLOUDFRONT-NO_WAF-01
title: CloudFront distribution has no AWS WAF web ACL associated
script: aws/live/cloudfront.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1188
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Create or reuse an AWS WAF (WAFv2) web ACL scoped to CloudFront
  and associate it with this distribution, starting from the AWS Managed
  Core rule group so common web exploits (SQL injection, known bad inputs)
  are filtered at the edge before ever reaching an origin. An unassociated
  distribution passes every request straight through with no rule evaluation
  of any kind.
references: CWE-1188
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/cloudfront-features.html

id: CLOUD-CLOUDFRONT-ORIGIN_EXPOSED-01
title: CloudFront S3 origin has no Origin Access Control or Origin Access Identity
script: aws/live/cloudfront.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Create an Origin Access Control (OAC; the current, SigV4-based
  mechanism - the legacy Origin Access Identity still works but AWS
  recommends OAC for new distributions) for this origin, attach it to the
  distribution, and restrict the S3 bucket policy to that OAC's principal.
  Without either, the bucket has to be reachable by more than just this
  distribution to serve content at all, which in practice usually means the
  bucket is public or trusts a far wider principal than "requests CloudFront
  itself forwarded".
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html

id: CLOUD-CLOUDFRONT-NO_LOGGING-01
title: CloudFront distribution standard logging is not enabled
script: aws/live/cloudfront.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-778
owasp: A09:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable standard access logging for this distribution and
  deliver the logs to a separate, restricted S3 bucket. Without it there is
  no per-request record of what CloudFront served at the edge, so a later
  investigation into a suspected abuse or content exposure has nothing to
  work from.
references: CWE-778
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/AccessLogs.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ECR / ECS / EKS - docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md CLOUD-25/26/27 (Tier 6,
# containers).  All six records below are `script: aws/live/<service>.sh`
# for the service owning them, exactly as every S3 record above is `script:
# aws/live/s3.sh` - one shared, append-only registry per the header note.
#
# NONE OF THESE SIX RECORDS CARRIES A `cis:` VALUE, AND THAT IS AN HONEST
# ABSENCE RATHER THAN A GAP LEFT FOR LATER.  `data/cis-mappings` names
# exactly one benchmark - CIS Amazon Web Services Foundations Benchmark
# v3.0.0 (see this file's own header on `cis:`, and docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §4)
# - and that benchmark's own numbering covers IAM (section 1), Storage
# (section 2: S3/EBS/RDS/EFS), Logging (section 3) and Networking (section
# 5) only; it has no ECR, ECS or EKS section at all.  A SEPARATE published
# benchmark exists for containers (CIS Amazon ECS Benchmark, CIS Amazon EKS
# Benchmark), but citing an id from either against a table that names only
# the Foundations Benchmark and its version would misattribute the finding
# exactly as docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §3 warns a version mismatch does - "a
# table that cannot say which [benchmark] edition a given control number was
# drawn from ... silently misattribute[s] every id in it the day a second
# [benchmark] is ever added" applies identically to a second BENCHMARK, not
# only a second version of the same one.  Adding that second benchmark to
# `data/cis-mappings` is a real, deliberate register change for whoever
# transcribes it by hand from the published PDF (docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §5) -
# not a thing to guess at from inside a check record.  Each finding still
# carries its CWE and OWASP category, exactly as S3's own three
# CIS-less records (encryption, versioning, logging) already do.

id: CLOUD-ECR-PUBLIC_REPOSITORY-01
title: ECR repository policy grants access to a wildcard principal
script: aws/live/ecr.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Review the repository's resource policy
  (`aws ecr get-repository-policy --repository-name <name>`) and remove the
  statement that grants an Allow to a wildcard Principal - either the bare
  string "*" or {"AWS": "*"}, both of which admit every AWS account, or an
  anonymous caller if nothing else fronts the pull. This is scoursh's own
  reading of the policy statement rather than an AWS-evaluated verdict: ECR
  has no equivalent of S3's get-bucket-policy-status, so confirm the finding
  against the full policy before acting on it. Scope the Principal to named
  account ids or roles, or remove the statement entirely if no cross-account
  pull was ever intended.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/repository-policies.html

id: CLOUD-ECR-SCAN_ON_PUSH_OFF-01
title: ECR repository does not scan images on push
script: aws/live/ecr.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1188
owasp: A06:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable scan-on-push for the repository (or a registry-wide
  scanning configuration covering it) so an image is checked for
  known-vulnerable packages the moment it is pushed, rather than discovered
  later by a separate audit or never at all. This is read directly off
  imageScanningConfiguration.scanOnPush; an absent configuration is reported
  the same as an explicitly-disabled one, since a repository created before
  scan-on-push existed carries no scanning either way.
references: CWE-1188
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/image-scanning.html

id: CLOUD-ECR-MUTABLE_TAGS-01
title: ECR repository allows image tags to be overwritten
script: aws/live/ecr.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-345
owasp: A08:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set the repository's image tag mutability to IMMUTABLE. With
  mutable tags (AWS's own default), a tag like :latest or :prod can be
  silently repointed to a different image after it has been reviewed or
  deployed, undermining any verification that trusted the tag rather than
  the image digest. A deployment pipeline that needs to move a tag should
  push a new one instead of overwriting an existing one.
references: CWE-345
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/image-tag-mutability.html

id: CLOUD-ECS-PUBLIC_SERVICE-01
title: ECS service assigns a public IP to its tasks
script: aws/live/ecs.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-668
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set the service's networkConfiguration.awsvpcConfiguration.
  assignPublicIp to DISABLED and route any outbound traffic the tasks need
  through a NAT gateway instead, unless the service genuinely must be
  directly reachable from the internet. This finding is a signal that the
  task's network interface CAN receive a public IPv4 address, not a
  confirmed internet-reachability verdict - actual reachability also
  depends on the subnet's route table and the attached security group,
  neither of which this check reads, so confirm both before deciding the
  exposure is (or is not) real.
references: CWE-668
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/task-networking-awsvpc.html

id: CLOUD-ECS-TASK_ROLE_OVERPERMISSIVE-01
title: ECS task role grants unrestricted access
script: aws/live/ecs.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Replace the wildcard statement (Action "*" on Resource "*") in
  the task role's inline policy with the specific actions and resource ARNs
  the application actually uses - review CloudTrail for the API calls it
  has really made if that is not already known. Every container that runs
  using this task definition inherits this role's full permission set at
  runtime, so an application-level compromise (a dependency vulnerability,
  an injection flaw) becomes an account-wide one. Only the task role's
  INLINE policies are read by this check; a managed (attached) policy
  granting the same wildcard is a real gap this check does not close.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/task-iam-roles.html

id: CLOUD-EKS-PUBLIC_ENDPOINT-01
title: EKS cluster API endpoint is reachable from outside the VPC
script: aws/live/eks.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-668
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Disable public access to the cluster's API endpoint
  (endpointPublicAccess: false) and reach it over the VPC via
  endpointPrivateAccess, or at minimum restrict publicAccessCidrs to the
  specific ranges that legitimately need it rather than the 0.0.0.0/0
  default. The API server is the single most powerful control point in the
  cluster - anyone who can reach it and present valid credentials can
  create or modify any workload in it.
references: CWE-668
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/cluster-endpoint.html

id: CLOUD-EKS-POD_ROLE_OVERPERMISSIVE-01
title: EKS node group IAM role grants unrestricted access
script: aws/live/eks.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Replace the wildcard statement (Action "*" on Resource "*") in
  the node group's inline role policy with the specific actions and
  resource ARNs the node's own workloads use, and adopt IAM Roles for
  Service Accounts (IRSA) for any pod that needs AWS API access beyond
  that. Every pod scheduled onto a node in this group that has NOT adopted
  its own IRSA binding inherits this NODE role's full permission set via
  the EC2 instance metadata service - this check evaluates the node
  group's role because a literal per-pod IRSA binding is recorded inside
  the cluster's own Kubernetes API server, which scoursh has no credential
  or network path to read; a cluster where every workload already has a
  narrowly-scoped IRSA role is not fully assessed by this check alone.
  Only the node role's INLINE policies are read; a managed (attached)
  policy granting the same wildcard is a real gap this check does not
  close.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/service-accounts.html
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/create-node-role.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUD-SNS-*, CLOUD-SQS-*, CLOUD-ACM-*, CLOUD-ROUTE53-*, CLOUD-BACKUP-*
# (docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md CLOUD-28/29/10/11/12) - owned by
# modules/cloud/aws/live/{sns,sqs,acm,route53,backup}.sh.  Appended to this
# shared, append-only registry per this file's own header above (a conflict
# here is resolved by keeping BOTH blocks, never by choosing a side).
#
# NONE OF THESE SEVEN RECORDS CARRIES A `cis:` VALUE, AND THAT IS AN HONEST
# ABSENCE RATHER THAN A GAP THIS TICKET LEFT.  This file's own header states
# the rule at length for S3's three uncited records: `cis:` is authored only
# where a real CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 control exists for the
# property being observed, and data/cis-mappings' benchmark-v3.0.0 catalog
# (sections 1 IAM, 2.1 S3, 2.2-2.4 EBS/RDS/EFS encryption, 3 logging/
# monitoring, 5 networking) contains no section for SNS, SQS, ACM, Route53 or
# AWS Backup at all - inventing a control id to satisfy a "must cite CIS"
# habit is precisely the overstated-coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15
# forbids.  A future ticket that transcribes a control genuinely covering one
# of these five services adds the row via docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §5 and the
# `cis:` line here in the same change.

id: CLOUD-SNS-PUBLIC_POLICY-01
title: SNS topic policy grants access to a wildcard principal
script: aws/live/sns.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove or narrow the statement in this topic's resource policy
  that grants Effect Allow to a wildcard Principal ("*" or {"AWS":"*"}) with
  no Condition. Name the specific accounts, roles or AWS services that need to
  publish to or subscribe from this topic, or add a Condition
  (aws:SourceArn/aws:SourceAccount for an AWS-service principal,
  aws:PrincipalOrgID for a cross-account grant scoped to your organisation)
  that genuinely narrows who the grant applies to. A wildcard principal with
  no condition lets any AWS account - and, for actions that do not require
  SigV4, an unauthenticated caller - use this topic.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-access-policy-use-cases.html

id: CLOUD-SNS-NO_ENCRYPTION-01
title: SNS topic has no server-side encryption configured
script: aws/live/sns.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set a KmsMasterKeyId on this topic - a customer-managed key for
  a separate, auditable key policy and CloudTrail key-usage events, or the AWS
  managed alias/aws/sns as the zero-cost baseline. Unlike S3 and SQS, SNS
  applies no default managed encryption to a new topic, so this is the
  topic's real, current state rather than an inherited default that happens
  to be safe.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-server-side-encryption.html

id: CLOUD-SQS-PUBLIC_POLICY-01
title: SQS queue policy grants access to a wildcard principal
script: aws/live/sqs.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-732
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove or narrow the statement in this queue's resource policy
  that grants Effect Allow to a wildcard Principal ("*" or {"AWS":"*"}) with
  no Condition. Name the specific accounts, roles or AWS services that need to
  send to or receive from this queue, or add a Condition that genuinely
  narrows the grant (aws:SourceArn for an AWS-service principal,
  aws:PrincipalOrgID for a cross-account grant scoped to your organisation). A
  wildcard principal with no condition lets any AWS account use this queue.
references: CWE-732
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-security-best-practices.html

id: CLOUD-SQS-NO_ENCRYPTION-01
title: SQS queue has no server-side encryption configured
script: aws/live/sqs.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable SQS-managed server-side encryption (SqsManagedSseEnabled)
  as the zero-cost baseline, or set a customer-managed KmsMasterKeyId where a
  separate, auditable key policy and CloudTrail key-usage events are
  required. This queue has neither configured, so messages in it are not
  encrypted at rest.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-server-side-encryption.html

id: CLOUD-ACM-EXPIRING-01
title: ACM certificate is nearing or past its expiry date
script: aws/live/acm.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-298
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Renew this certificate before it lapses. If it is ACM-issued
  with DNS or email validation, confirm the validation record still exists
  and the domain still resolves - a changed or removed validation CNAME is
  the commonest reason an ACM-managed certificate silently fails to
  auto-renew. If it is imported rather than ACM-issued, ACM cannot renew it
  automatically and it must be replaced with a new certificate manually. A
  certificate that lapses while still attached to a listener breaks TLS for
  every client of the service it terminates.
references: CWE-298
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/latest/userguide/managed-renewal.html

id: CLOUD-ROUTE53-DANGLING_RECORD-01
title: DNS record points to an S3 static-website endpoint for a bucket that does not exist
script: aws/live/route53.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-706
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove this DNS record if the site it served is retired, or
  recreate the S3 bucket it names (with static-website hosting configured) if
  it is not. AWS's S3 static-website hosting feature requires the serving
  bucket's name to exactly match the DNS name being served; while the bucket
  does not exist, ANY AWS account - not only yours - can create a bucket with
  that exact name and configure it as a website, taking over everything
  served at this DNS name. This check covers only the S3-website subdomain-
  takeover vector; a CNAME/ALIAS to a retired ELB, CloudFront distribution or
  third-party hosting provider is a related but separate risk this check does
  not evaluate.
references: CWE-706
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/dns-configuring-dangling-domain.html

id: CLOUD-BACKUP-NO_PLAN_COVERAGE-01
title: EBS volume has no AWS Backup recovery point
script: aws/live/backup.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Add this volume to a backup plan's resource selection - by its
  own ARN, by a tag it carries, or via an account-wide wildcard selection -
  and confirm a rule in that plan actually runs against it. A volume with no
  recovery point is unrecoverable if it is accidentally deleted, corrupted,
  or encrypted by a ransomware-style attack. If this volume is intentionally
  excluded (purely ephemeral scratch storage that is rebuilt rather than
  restored), no action is needed, but confirm that is a deliberate choice
  rather than an oversight in the backup plan's selection criteria.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/assigning-resources.html
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUD-OPENSEARCH-*, CLOUD-REDSHIFT-*, CLOUD-EFS-* - docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md
# CLOUD-17/18/19, the "data stores" tier's three §8.1 rows sharing one
# three-check shape: public access, no encryption at rest, no encryption in
# transit. Owned by modules/cloud/aws/live/opensearch.sh, redshift.sh and
# efs.sh respectively.
#
# WHY NONE OF THESE NINE RECORDS CITES A `cis` VALUE, EXCEPT ONE.  Following
# the S3 records' own rule above ("cis IS AUTHORED HERE ... AND ONLY WHERE A
# REAL CIS v3.0.0 CONTROL EXISTS"): the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0
# has no OpenSearch or Redshift section at all - its storage coverage (§2) is
# S3, EBS, RDS and EFS only - so none of the six OpenSearch/Redshift records
# below cites one, and inventing a control number for either service is
# exactly the overstated-coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 forbids.  EFS IS
# in scope (§2.4), but only for encryption at rest: `data/cis-mappings`
# carries `2.4.1`, "Ensure EFS volumes are encrypted at rest", which
# CLOUD-EFS-NO_ENCRYPTION_AT_REST-01 cites below - the benchmark's EFS
# coverage stops there, so EFS's own public-access and in-transit records
# cite nothing either, for the identical reason S3's own NO_VERSIONING/
# NO_LOGGING records above cite nothing.
#
# WHY THE THREE POLICY-HEURISTIC CHECKS (OpenSearch and EFS public access,
# EFS in-transit) ARE `confidence: medium` WHILE EVERY REDSHIFT CHECK AND
# EVERY ENCRYPTION-AT-REST CHECK IS `high`.  Redshift's `PubliclyAccessible`
# and `Encrypted` are direct API fields, and OpenSearch's/EFS's encryption
# flags are too - an exact read, the same `high` S3's own field-read checks
# earn.  OpenSearch and EFS have no `get-bucket-policy-status`-style
# evaluator API, so their public-access (and EFS's in-transit) checks read
# the raw policy document with modules/cloud/aws/engine.sh's
# `cloud_policy_is_wide_open`/`cloud_policy_denies_insecure_transport` - a
# heuristic over the policy's Principal/Condition shape, not AWS's own
# verdict, and the confidence field says so honestly rather than borrowing
# the same certainty an exact field read earns.
#
# The type tag is `config-read` on every record below, for the identical
# reason the S3 records above give: each check is one or two read-only AWS
# API calls and mutates nothing.

id: CLOUD-OPENSEARCH-PUBLIC_ACCESS-01
title: OpenSearch domain is on the public endpoint with a wide-open access policy
script: aws/live/opensearch.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Either attach the domain to a VPC (VPCOptions), which removes
  the public AWS-owned endpoint entirely, or narrow the access policy so its
  Principal names specific accounts, roles or a Condition that genuinely
  restricts the caller (aws:SourceVpc/aws:SourceVpce, or a source IP range)
  rather than admitting the wildcard "*". A domain with no VPC and a wide-open
  policy is reachable, unauthenticated, by any host on the internet that
  knows or guesses the endpoint.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/ac.html

id: CLOUD-OPENSEARCH-NO_ENCRYPTION_AT_REST-01
title: OpenSearch domain has no encryption at rest configured
script: aws/live/opensearch.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable encryption at rest with an AWS-owned or customer-managed
  KMS key. This setting cannot be changed on a running domain - migrate the
  data to a newly-created, encrypted domain (a snapshot restore or a reindex)
  rather than expecting an in-place toggle.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/encryption-at-rest.html

id: CLOUD-OPENSEARCH-NO_ENCRYPTION_IN_TRANSIT-01
title: OpenSearch domain has node-to-node transport encryption disabled
script: aws/live/opensearch.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Enable node-to-node encryption so traffic between the domain's
  own data nodes is encrypted in transit. This is a distinct setting from
  HTTPS-to-client (DomainEndpointOptions.EnforceHTTPS) and from encryption at
  rest, and like encryption at rest it cannot be toggled in place - plan a
  migration to a newly-created domain with the setting enabled.
references: CWE-319
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/ntn.html

id: CLOUD-REDSHIFT-PUBLIC_ACCESS-01
title: Redshift cluster is configured as publicly accessible
script: aws/live/redshift.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set the cluster's PubliclyAccessible option to false and reach
  it from outside the VPC through a VPN, Direct Connect, or a bastion/proxy
  host inside the VPC instead. A publicly accessible cluster is reachable
  from the internet whenever its security group and network ACLs also permit
  the connection, which is the ordinary case for a security group opened for
  a BI tool or an operator's own workstation.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/managing-clusters-vpc.html

id: CLOUD-REDSHIFT-NO_ENCRYPTION_AT_REST-01
title: Redshift cluster has no encryption at rest configured
script: aws/live/redshift.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Encryption at rest cannot be enabled on a running cluster - create
  a new, encrypted cluster from a snapshot of the existing one (or with KMS
  encryption enabled from the start) and switch client connections over to
  it, then decommission the unencrypted cluster.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/working-with-db-encryption.html

id: CLOUD-REDSHIFT-NO_ENCRYPTION_IN_TRANSIT-01
title: Redshift cluster parameter group does not require SSL
script: aws/live/redshift.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set require_ssl to true in the cluster's own parameter group and
  apply it. Without it, a client can connect and exchange query text and
  result rows in the clear even though the cluster supports TLS; setting the
  parameter does not itself disconnect existing plaintext sessions, so plan a
  client reconnect after applying it.
references: CWE-319
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/connecting-ssl-support.html

id: CLOUD-EFS-PUBLIC_ACCESS-01
title: EFS file system policy grants access to a wildcard principal
script: aws/live/efs.sh
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Scope the file system policy's Principal to specific accounts or
  roles, or add a Condition restricting elasticfilesystem:AccessedViaMountTarget
  and the expected VPC/account, rather than admitting the wildcard "*". A
  wide-open policy is not itself internet exposure - EFS has no public
  endpoint - but it means any principal that can reach a mount target,
  including one outside this account if network access is otherwise
  available, can mount the file system without being named on the policy.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/iam-access-control-nfs-efs.html

id: CLOUD-EFS-NO_ENCRYPTION_AT_REST-01
title: EFS file system has no encryption at rest configured
script: aws/live/efs.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 2.4.1
remediation: Encryption at rest can only be set when a file system is
  created - there is no in-place toggle. Create a new, encrypted file system
  and migrate the data onto it (AWS DataSync or an EFS-to-EFS copy), then
  redirect mount targets and decommission the unencrypted file system.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/encryption-at-rest.html

id: CLOUD-EFS-NO_ENCRYPTION_IN_TRANSIT-01
title: EFS file system policy does not require encryption in transit
script: aws/live/efs.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Add a Deny statement to the file system policy conditioned on
  Bool aws:SecureTransport=false, so every client is required to mount over
  the TLS-wrapped transport (mount.efs's -o tls option) rather than plain
  NFS. Without it, TLS is available but not enforced, and a client's own
  mount options - not this file system's own configuration - decide whether
  traffic is encrypted.
references: CWE-319
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/encryption-in-transit.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The following two records are owned by modules/cloud/aws/live/appsync.sh
# (docs/DESIGN.md §8.5; docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md CLOUD-23).  Appended to this
# shared, append-only registry per this file's own header rule.
#
# NEITHER RECORD CARRIES A `cis` VALUE, AND THAT IS AN HONEST ABSENCE RATHER
# THAN A GAP.  CIS Amazon Web Services Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 has no
# section for AppSync, or for any managed GraphQL service - its four sections
# cover IAM, storage (S3/EBS/RDS), logging/monitoring, and networking - so
# there is no control number to cite.  `data/cis-mappings`'s own header
# records the identical judgement for the three S3 checks that also cite
# nothing; inventing one here to satisfy a "must cite CIS" habit is the
# overstated-coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15 exists to forbid.  Neither
# record carries `tags: compliance` either, matching the rule those three S3
# checks already establish: that tag is carried by exactly the records that
# also carry a `cis` value.
#
# TWO IDS FOR TWO INDEPENDENT FACTS, PER §8.5'S OWN TEXT ("API-key auth in
# use and key expiry").  The CLOUD location profile carries no component
# naming the defect, so one id covering both an API's own default-auth choice
# and one of its keys' expiry would collide two unrelated findings about two
# different resources (the API itself; one API key) onto whichever
# fingerprint sorted first.

id: CLOUD-APPSYNC-API_KEY_DEFAULT_AUTH-01
title: AppSync GraphQL API's default authentication is a plain API key
script: aws/live/appsync.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-287
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Move the API's default authentication off API_KEY onto AWS_IAM
  (for first-party, SigV4-signing callers such as another AWS service or a
  backend you control) or Amazon Cognito user pools (for end users who need
  individual identity and field-level `@aws_auth` authorization). An API key
  is a static, bearer-style credential that carries no caller identity -
  anyone who holds it, however it reached them, gets whatever access the
  schema grants an API-key caller, with no per-caller authorization, no MFA,
  and no revocation short of deleting the key itself. If API_KEY is kept at
  all, reserve it for development use with a short-lived key and pair it with
  additional authentication providers so production traffic never depends on
  it.
references: CWE-287
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appsync/latest/devguide/security-authz.html

id: CLOUD-APPSYNC-API_KEY_LONG_EXPIRY-01
title: AppSync API key has a long, far-future expiration
script: aws/live/appsync.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-613
owasp: A07:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Reissue the key with a short lifetime - the 7-to-30-day range
  covers most legitimate uses, and AppSync's own maximum for any key is 365
  days from creation - and put a rotation process in front of whatever
  consumes it before the old key expires. The longer a static, unrotatable
  API key stays valid, the longer it remains useful if it is ever captured -
  copied out of a mobile app's decompiled binary, committed to a repository,
  or read straight out of a JavaScript bundle a browser downloaded. Where the
  consuming client can authenticate another way, move it off API_KEY
  authentication entirely (see CLOUD-APPSYNC-API_KEY_DEFAULT_AUTH-01) rather
  than only shortening this key's lifetime.
references: CWE-613
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appsync/latest/devguide/security-authz.html
